card(8,1, sub="Day 1: Show, don’t tell",
 focus="Real characters have specific feelings, and the writer never names them. Instead you use three kinds of detail: something the body does, something the senses catch, and something the kid says. This is the most useful trick in the whole project.",
 anne="In Chapter 6, Marilla decides to keep Anne. She never says so in that scene. You know it from what she does with Mrs. Blewett.",
 watch=dict(intro="Sam takes one feeling word and replaces it with three kinds of detail.",
   before="Mateo was scared.",
   after="Mateo’s knees wobbled like wet paper. The whistle screamed and he flinched. “You can do this,” he whispered. “You can do this.”",
   notice="A body detail (knees like wet paper), a sensory detail (the whistle screamed), and dialogue the kid says to himself. Three kinds of detail doing one job."),
 tool=dict(title="Detail chart", intro="Four rows. Use at least two whenever you’re about to write a feeling word.",
   table=[["Kind of detail","Sam’s, for Mateo"],["Body (show feelings)","knees wobbled like wet paper"],["Sensory (see / hear / smell / touch)","the whistle screamed"],["Dialogue","“You can do this,” he whispered"],["Appearance","his jersey hung loose on his thin shoulders"]],
   yourturn="Body: ____ · Sensory: ____ · Dialogue: ____ · Appearance: ____"),
 frames=["[Kid]’s [body part] felt like ___.","The ___ [sound verb]-ed and [kid] ___.","“___,” [kid] whispered / cried / laughed.","[Kid]’s [thing] hung / sat / leaned ___."],
 ms="Still planning. Nothing to draft yet.",
 prompt="Write ONE sentence about your kid that uses a feeling word (scared, happy, angry, embarrassed). Then rewrite it with no feeling word: a body detail, a sensory detail, and one line of dialogue.",
 ph="Feeling word version: ___ / Rewrite: ___")

